          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Choose the Battery Platform Before the Mower
          </h2>

          <p>
            The mower is a five-to-ten year purchase. The battery platform is a fifteen-year one, because every cordless tool you add afterward is either compatible or a second set of batteries and chargers. That makes the platform decision more consequential than the mower decision, and it is the one most buyers make by accident.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Batteries cost more than the tools.</strong> A large high-capacity pack frequently costs as much as the bare tool it powers, sometimes more. Once you own two or three packs on a platform, switching brands means either abandoning that investment or duplicating it, which is why platform lock-in is real and worth thinking about deliberately.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Voltage is not interchangeable, and neither are brands.</strong> There is no cross-brand standard. An EGO battery does not fit a Ryobi tool, and a 40V pack does not fit a 56V tool even within the same brand. Some brands maintain multiple incompatible lines simultaneously, which is worth checking before assuming a battery you own will work.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Consider tool breadth, not just the mower.</strong> Ask what else you will want in five years: a string trimmer, a leaf blower, a chainsaw, a hedge trimmer, a snow blower, a pressure washer. Platforms that cover all of those turn each subsequent purchase into a bare-tool price. Platforms with a narrow lineup do not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Think about supply longevity.</strong> A platform sold through many retailers with a long track record is a safer bet for finding replacement batteries in eight years than one tied to a single chain or recently launched. This is the least glamorous consideration and one of the most practically important.
          </p>

