        <!-- SECTION 2: Voltage, Amp-Hours, and What Actually Predicts Performance -->
        <div id="voltage-and-runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Voltage, Amp-Hours, and What Actually Predicts Performance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery specifications are marketed confusingly, and two numbers get conflated constantly. Separating them makes comparison shopping far easier.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Voltage Predicts Power</h3>

          <p>
            Voltage determines how much power the system can deliver at once, which shows up as torque: whether the mower holds blade speed in thick grass and whether the drive wheels can pull the machine up a slope. Higher voltage platforms, 56V and above, consistently outperformed 40V mowers in our thick-grass and hill tests. Within reasonable limits, voltage predicts capability.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Amp-Hours Predict Runtime</h3>

          <p>
            Amp-hours measure how much charge the pack holds, which determines how long it runs. A 56V 7.5Ah pack runs considerably longer than a 56V 4.0Ah pack on the same mower, with identical cutting power. Comparing amp-hours across different voltages is meaningless, though, because total energy is voltage multiplied by amp-hours.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watt-Hours Is the Honest Comparison</h3>

          <p>
            Multiply voltage by amp-hours to get watt-hours, which is the actual energy stored and the only figure comparable across platforms. A 40V 6.0Ah pack holds 240 watt-hours; a 56V 5.0Ah pack holds 280. That comparison tells you something real, where comparing 6.0Ah to 5.0Ah tells you the opposite of the truth.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Discount the Advertised Runtime</h3>

          <p>
            Manufacturer runtime figures assume dry grass, mulching mode, a high cutting height, and a new battery. Real conditions typically produce 60 to 75 percent of the stated number. Estimate your lawn at roughly 30 minutes per quarter acre with a 21-inch deck, compare against the discounted figure, and buy a second battery if the margin is thin.
          </p>

