        <div id="drive-systems" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Push, Front-Wheel Drive, or Rear-Wheel Drive
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cordless mowers come in all three configurations, and the right one depends almost entirely on terrain rather than lawn size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Push Mowers</h3>

          <p>
            No drive system at all, which means less weight, lower cost, longer runtime from the same battery since no energy goes to the wheels, and one fewer component to fail. On a flat lawn under a quarter acre, a push mower is genuinely the sensible choice, and battery mowers are light enough that pushing them is far less work than pushing a gas mower of the same deck size.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Front-Wheel Drive</h3>

          <p>
            The cheaper self-propel layout. It makes pivoting easy, since lifting the front wheels disengages the drive and lets you turn on the spot. The weakness is traction: as you push forward, weight shifts rearward off the drive wheels, so on any real slope the front wheels lose grip and you end up pushing anyway. Fine on flat ground, frustrating on hills.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rear-Wheel Drive</h3>

          <p>
            Puts the drive wheels where the mower weight naturally concentrates during use, which produces markedly better traction on slopes and uneven ground. In our hill testing every rear-wheel-drive mower climbed without assistance while front-wheel-drive machines needed help. It costs more and is slightly less nimble for tight pivots, and on any yard with genuine elevation change it is worth the difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            One point that surprises people coming from gas: a cordless push mower is far less work to push than a gas push mower of the same deck size, because there is no heavy engine block sitting on top of the deck. Several battery push mowers weigh 20 to 30 pounds less than their gas equivalents, which is enough that many owners who assumed they needed self-propulsion find they do not. If your lawn is flat, try a push model before paying for a drive system you may never miss.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Speed-Sensing Drive Systems</h3>

          <p>
            Toro Personal Pace and Snapper Step Sense read pressure on the handle and match wheel speed to your walking pace automatically. There is no dial to set and no thinking involved, and after a brief adjustment period most people prefer it to a fixed speed setting, especially on varied terrain where your natural pace changes anyway.
          </p>
        </div>

