            5. Sizing the Mower to Your Lawn
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Under a Quarter Acre, Flat</h3>

          <p>
            A 40V or 48V push mower with a 20 to 21-inch deck is genuinely sufficient, and the money saved over a self-propelled high-voltage machine is better kept. Runtime will not be a constraint, weight stays low, and storage is easier. Prioritize cut quality and a comfortable handle over voltage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Quarter to Half Acre</h3>

          <p>
            Step up to 56V or 60V with a 21 or 22-inch deck and self-propulsion. This is the sweet spot of the category, where runtime comfortably covers the lawn in one session and there is enough torque in reserve for damp grass and the occasional missed week.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Half Acre and Up</h3>

          <p>
            An 80V platform with a large pack, or a 56V mower with a second battery. Runtime becomes the binding constraint before power does on most large lawns, and swapping a battery mid-mow is a thirty-second interruption rather than a problem, provided you own a second pack.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Any Lawn With Real Slope</h3>

          <p>
            Prioritize rear-wheel drive over everything else, including deck size and even runtime. A mower that cannot pull itself uphill turns self-propulsion into dead weight, and no amount of battery capacity compensates for a drive system that slips.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Above an Acre</h3>

          <p>
            A walk-behind mower of any power source becomes a two-hour job at this size. A riding mower or a zero-turn is the appropriate tool, and battery-powered riding mowers now exist at the consumer level if you want to stay cordless.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Making Your Batteries Last Longer -->
        <div id="battery-care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Making Your Batteries Last Longer
          </h2>

          <p>
            The battery is the most expensive component on a cordless mower and the only one that wears out on a schedule. A few habits meaningfully extend its usable life, and most owners do the opposite of all of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store at a partial charge, not full.</strong> Lithium cells age fastest when held at a high state of charge. Storing packs at roughly half charge for extended periods, particularly over winter, measurably slows capacity loss compared to leaving them on the charger.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep them out of heat.</strong> Heat is the single biggest accelerator of lithium degradation, and an uninsulated garage in summer regularly exceeds the temperatures where damage occurs. Bringing packs indoors, or at least keeping them off a concrete floor in direct sun, is the highest-value habit here.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Let a hot pack cool before charging.</strong> A battery pulled straight off a mower after 45 minutes of hard cutting is warm, and charging it immediately adds more heat. Most quality chargers will wait for the pack to cool, but if yours starts immediately, give it fifteen minutes first.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not run packs completely flat repeatedly.</strong> Modern battery management systems protect against true over-discharge, but habitually running to shutdown is harder on cells than swapping at a low reserve. If you own two packs, swapping before full depletion is better for both.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Expect gradual decline, not sudden failure.</strong> A pack does not stop working, it slowly holds less charge. Most people notice around year four or five that a mow that used to take one battery now needs a swap. That is normal, and it is usually the point where buying a fresh pack restores the mower to like-new performance for far less than a new machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep the charger somewhere with airflow. Chargers generate heat while working, and a unit crammed into a closed cabinet or buried behind boxes runs hotter, which slows charging and adds thermal stress to the pack sitting in it. A shelf with clear air around it is all that is needed.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Winter Storage Mistake</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Leaving batteries on the charger all winter, or leaving them in a freezing garage at full charge, are the two fastest ways to lose capacity. Charge to roughly half, bring the packs inside to a closet or basement, and check them once mid-winter. Ten minutes of effort in November is worth a full season of runtime years later.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Frequently Asked Questions -->
