            7. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best battery powered lawn mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The EGO Power+ LM2135SP is the best overall choice for most lawns, combining gas-comparable torque, rear-wheel drive that handles slopes, and the strongest battery platform in cordless outdoor tools. For value, the Ryobi 40V HP does nearly as much for less on flat ground, and for a genuinely hilly yard the Snapper XD 82V has the best traction we tested.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do battery lawn mowers run on one charge?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A quality cordless mower with a large pack runs 45 to 60 minutes in real conditions, covering roughly a quarter to half acre. Manufacturer figures assume dry grass, mulching mode, and a new battery, so real-world runtime usually lands at 60 to 75 percent of the advertised number. Bagging clippings and cutting damp grass both reduce it further.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does higher voltage mean a better mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Voltage predicts power rather than runtime. Higher-voltage platforms deliver more torque, so they hold blade speed in thick grass and drive better up slopes. Runtime is determined by amp-hours, and total energy is voltage multiplied by amp-hours, which is why comparing amp-hours across different voltages is misleading.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do lawn mower batteries last before replacement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Quality lithium packs are rated for several hundred charge cycles and typically deliver five or more years of normal residential service before capacity loss becomes noticeable. Storing them at partial charge in a temperate place rather than fully charged in a hot garage extends that meaningfully. Replacements generally cost 150 to 350 dollars depending on the platform.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a second battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If your realistic mowing time is within about fifteen minutes of the mower discounted runtime, yes. Two moderate packs usually cost less than one very large pack, allow a thirty-second swap mid-mow, and provide redundancy as the first pack ages. On lawns approaching half an acre it is the more practical purchase.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are battery mowers powerful enough for thick grass?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Platforms at 56V and above handle thick and damp grass well, holding blade speed without significant bogging. Lower-voltage 40V mowers manage routine grass fine but slow noticeably in heavy growth and may need a second pass at a higher cutting height. For consistently thick or tall grass, an 80V platform closes almost all of the remaining gap to gas.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Cordless Mower Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cordless mower regret is almost always about sizing or platform choice rather than about picking a bad machine.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Picking the Mower Before the Platform</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Buying whichever mower is on sale locks you into a battery ecosystem for the next decade. If that platform has a narrow tool lineup or unreliable battery availability, every subsequent cordless purchase costs you more. Decide which platform you want to live with first, then buy the best mower on it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Advertised Runtime</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Published runtime figures are measured under ideal conditions that do not resemble a real lawn on a damp Saturday. Assume 60 to 75 percent of the stated number, compare against your actual mowing time, and buy a second battery if the margin is thin. Running out mid-lawn is the single most common cordless complaint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Front-Wheel Drive for a Sloped Yard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On a slope, weight shifts backward off front drive wheels exactly when you need traction most, so you end up pushing a heavy mower uphill anyway. If your yard has real elevation change, rear-wheel drive is not an upgrade, it is the requirement.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Batteries in a Hot Garage All Summer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Heat and a high state of charge are the two things that degrade lithium cells fastest, and a summer garage delivers both. Store packs at roughly half charge somewhere temperate and bring them indoors for winter. This costs nothing and adds years to the most expensive part of the mower.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

